\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

We evaluate vision token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three connector families on the LIBERO benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero}. LIBERO provides four task suites of increasing complexity: Object (object manipulation), Spatial (spatial reasoning), Goal (goal-conditioned tasks), and Long (long-horizon multi-step tasks). Each suite contains 10 tasks evaluated over 50 episodes, yielding 500 episodes per condition.

Four models are evaluated in closed-loop robotic control: $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B parameters, linear projection with $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling, 768 tokens from three cameras, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055), X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}, 50 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151), OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, three-layer MLP projector, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215), and SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M, pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding, 128 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314). Two additional models are analyzed via offline metrics only: $\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, linear projection, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.038) and OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} (7B, three-layer MLP, 512 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.219). All six architectures contribute to the CV diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:cv_sensitivity}). Table~\ref{tab:model_properties} summarizes model properties and connector details.

We follow the pruning protocol of Section~\ref{sec:pruning_protocol} at retention ratios $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, reporting mean success rate (SR) over 500 episodes with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{Two-Axis Framework Overview}
\label{sec:two_axis}

The pruning response depends on token independence imposed by the connector and task complexity (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; token-entangling connectors exhibit sharp, task-dependent collapse.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pruning_overview.pdf}
\caption{Pruning degradation on LIBERO-Object (L2 norm selection). Four VLAs span a complete degradation spectrum: $\pi_{0.5}$ maintains ${\geq}$98\% through $k{=}0.85$, OpenVLA degrades linearly, X-VLA cliffs at $k{=}0.7$, SmolVLA collapses to 0\% at $k{=}0.95$.}
\label{fig:pruning_overview}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents closed-loop success rates across four models, four LIBERO suites, two pruning methods, and four retention ratios. The data reveal four qualitatively distinct response patterns spanning the connector spectrum (Figure~\ref{fig:pruning_overview}).

\textbf{$\pi_{0.5}$ (linear, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055).} On Object, L2 selection yields a plateau (${\geq}$98.0\%) through $k{=}0.85$, then drops to 55.0\% at $k{=}0.7$. Random selection shows a different trajectory (74.0\% at $k{=}0.95$, non-monotonic thereafter). The method gap reaches 89.2\,pp at $k{=}0.9$ (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Low-CV models are not pruning-immune but uniformly critical: the choice of \emph{which} tokens to remove matters more than how many.

\textbf{X-VLA (linear + BART encoder, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151).} X-VLA exhibits threshold-graded behavior with a pronounced interaction between retention ratio and task complexity. On Object, L2 preserves ${\geq}$99.0\% through $k{=}0.9$, then falls sharply with non-monotonic recovery (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). The same model at $k{=}0.9$ achieves only 70.2\% on Goal, a 29.0\,pp cross-task gap driven entirely by task complexity. On Long, removing just 3 of 50 tokens ($k{=}0.95$) collapses SR to 47.0\% (L2) and 48.2\% (random); the 1.2\,pp method gap amid 47.0\,pp degradation confirms the BART encoder distributes information uniformly across tokens even as it amplifies compositional task demands.

\textbf{OpenVLA (MLP, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215).} OpenVLA reveals that even within a single token-independent architecture, the optimal scoring method is suite-dependent (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). At $k{=}0.9$ on Object, L2 outperforms random by $+$7.8\,pp (70.6\% vs.\ 62.8\%), consistent with the MLP projector's genuine importance gradient; attention-based scoring is worst at 61.4\% ($-$12.6\,pp from baseline), suggesting that attention weights misidentify salient tokens for object manipulation. On Spatial, the ranking inverts: attention is best at 80.4\% ($+$0.4\,pp, lossless), random near-baseline at 79.8\%, while L2 \emph{underperforms} at 75.0\% ($-$5.0\,pp). On Goal, attention achieves 73.2\% ($-$1.6\,pp, near-lossless), L2 matches baseline (74.8\%), and random anomalously exceeds it (79.6\%). On Long, all three methods converge (L2: 43.6\%, random: 46.8\%, attention: 44.0\%), with method gaps ${\leq}$3.2\,pp amid 6--9\,pp baseline degradation. This within-model crossover---attention worst on Object ($-$12.6\,pp) yet best on Spatial ($+$0.4\,pp), L2 best on Object yet worst on Spatial---demonstrates that no scoring method is universally safe, even within a single connector type.

\textbf{SmolVLA (pixel-shuffle, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314).} SmolVLA collapses to near-zero SR (${\leq}$6.6\%) across all four suites, both pruning methods, and all retention ratios including $k{=}0.95$ where only 6 of 128 tokens are removed. This catastrophic cliff is detailed in Section~\ref{sec:pixel_shuffle}.

\textbf{Task complexity amplification.} Multi-step tasks amplify degradation in a connector-dependent manner. Under random pruning at $k{=}0.9$, X-VLA loses 1.4\,pp on Object but 27.8\,pp on Goal ($20{\times}$ amplification). Token-entangling connectors show sharper amplification because compositional tasks require information integration across the cross-token dependencies that pruning disrupts (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_suite_extended}).

